{"sections":{"image-banner":{"type":"image-banner","blocks":{"heading":{"type":"heading","settings":{"heading":"Behind the cups","heading_size":"h1","heading_tag":"h2"}}},"block_order":["heading"],"settings":{"image":"shopify:\/\/shop_images\/Untitled_design_12.jpg","image_position":"center center","text_box_position":"middle-center","text_alignment":"center","image_ratio":"500px","show_text_box":false,"full_width":false,"show_divider":false,"mobile_text_alignment":"center","mobile_image_ratio":"200px","stack_images_on_mobile":false,"show_text_below":true,"colors_button_label":"#1a1b18","colors_button_background":"#ffffff","colors_text":"#ffffff","colors_overlay":"#000000","colors_overlay_opacity":28,"padding_top":0,"padding_bottom":36}},"main":{"type":"main-page","disabled":true,"settings":{"narrow":true,"padding_top":60,"padding_bottom":72}},"rich-text-0":{"type":"rich-text","blocks":{"text":{"type":"text","settings":{"text":"<p>Angela Kornas worked in the swimwear industry for 15 years. One thing that remained consistent in all swimsuit brands, no matter the quality and price point is that the removable bra inserts that came with them were truly horrendous. For over 25 years, there was no change in insert design. The flimsy pieces of foam mainly caused women frustration with the flipping, shifting and bunching with the grand finale of ending up at the bottom of washing machines. <\/p>","text_size":"typeset","secondary_color":false}}},"block_order":["text"],"settings":{"text_alignment":"left","mobile_text_alignment":"left","horizontal_content":false,"narrow":true,"show_divider":false,"use_custom_colors":false,"colors_button_label":"#ffffff","colors_button_background":"#1a1b18","colors_text":"#212326","colors_background":"#ffffff","gradient_background":"","padding_top":36,"padding_bottom":36}},"rich-text-1":{"type":"rich-text","blocks":{"text":{"type":"text","settings":{"text":"","text_size":"typeset","secondary_color":false}}},"block_order":["text"],"settings":{"t